{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>A woman when she emits seed and gives birth to a male.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Niddah 31a): if the man emits seed first — she gives birth to a female; if the woman emits seed first — she gives birth to a male. And the commentators wrote that this is a natural matter. And it is difficult for me: what need is there to inform us of this matter in this parashah, which speaks of the impurity of a woman who gives birth — seven days for a male and fourteen for a female? And further, what relevance does the commandment of circumcision have here? Even though we could say that the Torah wished to give a reason why circumcision was set on the eighth day — namely, so that not everyone be rejoicing while his father and mother are grieving (Niddah 31b), for she has a seven-day impurity — nevertheless, in order to settle all the difficulties in a single path, I say that all of this follows naturally from the primordial sin, as it is written: 'and she shall be purified from the source of her blood' — for the primordial sin of Chavah is an opened source for these impure bloods, for sin-offering and for menstruation, as we find in Eiruvin, chapter HaMotzi Tefillin (100a): Rav Yitzchak bar Avdimi said: 'I will greatly multiply your sorrow' — these are two drops of blood, the blood of menstruation and the blood of virginity, etc. And so it implies in the Yerushalmi, chapter BaMeh Madlikin (Shabbat 6): that the sin of Chavah is a source for the blood of menstruation. Therefore it says: 'and she shall be purified from the source of her blood' — for all women need purification on account of the first sin, from which impurity and pollution spread forth into the world, and it caused all those born to have a seven-day impurity. For had man not sinned, he would have been like an angel of Elohim, above the array of the seven moving stars; and through his sin the spirituality was removed from him and he fell under the dominion of that array, from which side impurity was drawn to man. For every material thing falls under the number of the seven moving stars and the seven days of Creation, while every eighth thing is spiritual, for it is above seven, as explained above in parashat Shemini (9a)." "And this is the reason for the seven-day impurity of the woman who gives birth: for all of this is drawn to her from the primordial sin, and from her the impurity spread to the male child that is born — because when a woman emits seed first she gives birth to a male. If so, the woman's seed is primary in the male child that is born, and there is no concern in him for the father's seed, for it is secondary to the woman's seed. Accordingly, the seven-day impurity was also impressed upon the male child from his mother — and this is the orlah, which possesses impurity, for our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Pesachim 92a): one who separates from the orlah is as one who separates from the grave. Therefore specifically on the eighth day the newborn is circumcised, for just as his mother's purification is on the eighth day, so too the newborn's purification from her — for at any rate he cannot be without a seven-day impurity just like his mother, who has a seven-day impurity, and their purification is on the eighth day, which is spiritual. But the female — in her the man's seed is primary, for when the man emits seed first she gives birth to a female. Just as the male has no seven-day impurity — for the serpent cast pollution specifically upon Chavah and not upon Adam — so too the female child that is born has no impurity whatsoever. And this serves well to respond to the sectarians who say: if the male is rectified by removal of the orlah, with what shall the female be purified?" "And the reason for the two-week impurity for the woman herself when giving birth to a female is as follows: it is just that the woman should have a double impurity corresponding to the two females. For each female in and of herself draws to herself a seven-day impurity from the primordial sin upon all the females in the world; therefore it is fitting that she be impure fourteen days — seven for herself and another seven for her daughter, for she has added impurity in the world upon her own impurity. And from this same reason follows the formation of the male in forty days and that of the female in eighty days. For it is known that at the time when the seed is hotter, then it hastens to mature more quickly and to become a fetus in the full womb. And it is known that the woman's seed is hotter than the man's seed, for the woman emits the redness in it, and all redness inclines more toward heat, while the father emits the whiteness in it, and all whiteness inclines more toward cold — as blood and snow testify, and fire and water. Therefore it is stated (Genesis 3:16): 'ואל אישך תשוקתך' — and our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Ketubot 86a): more than the man desires to marry, the woman desires to be married, because there is in the woman greater natural heat that inclines toward desire. Therefore the male, born from the woman's seed which is hotter, has his formation completed quickly within forty days. But the female is born from the man's seed, which is cold and does not mature quickly; therefore the formation of the female is not completed until eighty days. And this is the reason for the impurity of forty days for a male and eighty for a female. However, according to the opinion of our Rabbis of blessed memory, the formation of both male and female is forty days, and the reason for forty and eighty is that every female is cold and moist; therefore her mother needs a long time to cleanse herself from the abundance of moistures imprinted in her from the female, which is not so with a male."], :book :leviticus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {0 "The phrase 'לחטאת ולנדה' could be read as 'for a sin-offering and for menstruation' or 'for sin and for menstruation'; rendered literally to preserve the ambiguity.", 1 "The term 'המינים' (heretics/sectarians) is a standard rabbinic designation, here likely referring to Christian interlocutors who challenged the lack of a female covenant; transliterated concept preserved as 'sectarians.'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:36:13.144065871Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}